To simplify the expression $\left(\frac{100}{101}\right)^{-3}\left(\frac{100}{101}\right)^{3}$, we can use the property of exponents that states when you multiply two powers with the same base, you add their exponents. Here, the base is $\frac{100}{101}$, and the exponents are $-3$ and $3$. Therefore, we have:
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{100}{101}\right)^{-3}\left(\frac{100}{101}\right)^{3} &= \left(\frac{100}{101}\right)^{-3+3} \\
&= \left(\frac{100}{101}\right)^{0}.
\end{align*}
Any non-zero number raised to the power of 0 is 1. Thus, the simplified form of the expression is $\boxed{1}$</think> <answer>1</answer>

